    "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Disaster_Response_Logistics\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)  # Set time limit to 300 seconds\n\n    # Data\n    depots = list(range(3))  # D0..D2\n    units = list(range(4))  # U0..U3\n    supplies = list(range(3))  # S0..S2\n    days = list(range(1))  # Day0..Day0\n\n    demands = [[[29.82], [33.54], [32.03]], [[37.01], [11.15], [39.19]], [[35.48], [48.48], [30.86]], [[35.5], [28.91], [45.16]]]\n    depot_supplies = [[[43.31], [52.0], [48.55]], [[46.77], [52.02], [62.86]], [[50.64], [49.0], [56.28]]]\n    transport_costs = [[3.65, 3.2, 3.92, 1.12], [4.98, 2.09, 2.11, 4.78], [3.79, 3.93, 1.58, 3.39]]\n    risk_costs = [[129.59, 151.31, 145.66, 71.9], [137.95, 167.04, 167.71, 121.84], [80.26, 64.25, 165.18, 196.36]]\n    supply_volumes = [1.31, 0.62, 1.93]\n    penalty_costs = [96.35, 80.68, 50.6]\n    vehicle_cost = 10.0\n    vehicle_capacity = 100.0\n    total_vehicles = 20\n\n    # Decision variables\n    # Flow_D{d}_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}: Amount of supply s from depot d to unit u on day t\n    flows = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                for s in supplies:\n                    flows[(d, u, s, t)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Flow_D{d}_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Slack_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}: Shortage of supply s at unit u on day t\n    slacks = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for u in units:\n            for s in supplies:\n                slacks[(u, s, t)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Slack_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Veh_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}: Number of vehicles on route d->u on day t\n    vehicles = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                vehicles[(d, u, t)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.INTEGER, name=f\"Veh_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Route_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}: Route d->u is active on day t (binary)\n    routes = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                routes[(d, u, t)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Route_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize total cost (transport + penalty + vehicle + risk)\n    obj = quicksum(\n        transport_costs[d][u] * flows[(d, u, s, t)]\n        for t in days for d in depots for u in units for s in supplies\n    )\n    obj += quicksum(\n        penalty_costs[s] * slacks[(u, s, t)]\n        for t in days for u in units for s in supplies\n    )\n    obj += quicksum(\n        vehicle_cost * vehicles[(d, u, t)]\n        for t in days for d in depots for u in units\n    )\n    obj += quicksum(\n        risk_costs[d][u] * routes[(d, u, t)]\n        for t in days for d in depots for u in units\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Depot capacity constraints: Total outflow <= supply\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for s in supplies:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    quicksum(flows[(d, u, s, t)] for u in units) <= depot_supplies[d][s][t],\n                    name=f\"DepotCap_D{d}_S{s}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Demand constraints: Inflow + slack >= demand\n    for t in days:\n        for u in units:\n            for s in supplies:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    quicksum(flows[(d, u, s, t)] for d in depots) + slacks[(u, s, t)] >= demands[u][s][t],\n                    name=f\"Demand_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Vehicle capacity constraints: Total volume <= vehicles * capacity\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    quicksum(supply_volumes[s] * flows[(d, u, s, t)] for s in supplies) <= vehicle_capacity * vehicles[(d, u, t)],\n                    name=f\"VehCap_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Route activation constraints: vehicles <= total_vehicles * route\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    vehicles[(d, u, t)] <= total_vehicles * routes[(d, u, t)],\n                    name=f\"RouteAct_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Fleet size constraints: Total vehicles <= total_vehicles\n    for t in days:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(vehicles[(d, u, t)] for d in depots for u in units) <= total_vehicles,\n            name=f\"FleetSize_Day{t}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Situation:\nA severe storm system has disrupted ground lines of communication across the district. Landslides and high water have cut or degraded multiple corridors, and civil order remains fragile in several sectors. Supply Depot Alpha (rear area), Supply Depot Bravo (central hub), and Supply Depot Charlie (forward cache) hold mixed stocks of potable water, medical kits, and shelter materials. Priority receiving units include Field Hospital Delta, IDP Camp Echo, District Clinic Foxtrot, and Relief Battalion Headquarters. Movement along the valley corridor and riverside approaches is constrained by debris, unstable embankments, and security threats. Engineer and security elements can clear and secure selected lanes, but doing so requires deliberate activation and support.\n\nMission:\nMove life-saving relief supplies from depots to designated receiving units during the current operational period, maximizing demand fulfillment while minimizing total operational cost (sum of transport costs, shortage penalties, per-vehicle deployment costs, and fixed route security risk costs).\n\nExecution/Concept:\n- Sources and limits: Each depot will issue commodities up to its available stock by supply type and day. No depot may dispatch more than it holds.\n- Demand and shortfalls: Each receiving unit has stated requirements by supply type. Controlled shortfalls are permitted only where unavoidable; any unmet demand incurs a penalty that reflects operational impact and will be used sparingly.\n- Movement and capacity: Shipments are consolidated into vehicle loads. Each supply type has a defined volumetric footprint. The total loaded volume moved from any depot to any unit may not exceed the aggregate carrying capacity of vehicles assigned to that lane.\n- Route activation and risk: Vehicles may only travel on lanes that are formally activated. Critically, activating any depot-to-unit route (sending any vehicles on that lane) incurs a fixed Risk Cost representing security overhead (escort teams, route clearance, coordination), regardless of how many vehicles subsequently use the lane. This is separate from per-vehicle deployment cost and per-lane transport cost.\n- Fleet management: Vehicle assignments across all routes are capped by the available fleet. Each activated lane can carry only the number of vehicles assigned to it, and the sum of vehicles across all lanes may not exceed the fleet total.\n- Cost drivers: Transport costs vary by lane and reflect distance, terrain, and congestion. Vehicle deployment has a per-vehicle cost. Unmet demand carries a penalty cost by supply and destination. Fixed Risk Costs apply to each lane that is activated, independent of volume moved.\n- Prioritization: Medical and water deliveries to life-threatening gaps are prioritized. Lane activation decisions will weigh the fixed risk overhead against the marginal benefit of opening additional corridors. Where multiple depots can serve a unit, allocate flows to minimize total cost while respecting stocks and capacities.\n- Command and control: The Logistics Coordination Cell will publish a convoy tasking order identifying activated routes, vehicle allocations by lane, and commodity loading plans. Security and engineer teams will align with activated lanes for clearance and escort; lanes not activated will not receive traffic.\n\nAnnex A - Logistics Data:\nUnit Demands (Units x Supplies):\nUnit\\Supply | S 0 | S 1 | S 2\n-----------------------------\nU 0        |  29.82 |  33.54 |  32.03\nU 1        |  37.01 |  11.15 |  39.19\nU 2        |  35.48 |  48.48 |  30.86\nU 3        |  35.50 |  28.91 |  45.16\nDepot Supplies (Depots x Supplies):\nDepot\\Supply | S 0 | S 1 | S 2\n------------------------------\nD 0        |  43.31 |  52.00 |  48.55\nD 1        |  46.77 |  52.02 |  62.86\nD 2        |  50.64 |  49.00 |  56.28\nTransport Costs (Depot x Unit):\nDepot\\Unit | U 0 | U 1 | U 2 | U 3\n----------------------------------\nD 0        |   3.65 |   3.20 |   3.92 |   1.12\nD 1        |   4.98 |   2.09 |   2.11 |   4.78\nD 2        |   3.79 |   3.93 |   1.58 |   3.39\nRisk Costs (Depot x Unit):\nDepot\\Unit | U 0 | U 1 | U 2 | U 3\n----------------------------------\nD 0        | 129.59 | 151.31 | 145.66 |  71.90\nD 1        | 137.95 | 167.04 | 167.71 | 121.84\nD 2        |  80.26 |  64.25 | 165.18 | 196.36\nPenalty Costs per Unit Shortage: [Supply S0: 96.35, Supply S1: 80.68, Supply S2: 50.6]\nVolume per Supply Unit: [Supply S0: 1.31, Supply S1: 0.62, Supply S2: 1.93]\nFleet Parameters: Cost 10.0, Capacity 100.0, Total 20",
    "LLM_description_template": "Situation:\nA severe storm system has disrupted ground lines of communication across the district. Landslides and high water have cut or degraded multiple corridors, and civil order remains fragile in several sectors. Supply Depot Alpha (rear area), Supply Depot Bravo (central hub), and Supply Depot Charlie (forward cache) hold mixed stocks of potable water, medical kits, and shelter materials. Priority receiving units include Field Hospital Delta, IDP Camp Echo, District Clinic Foxtrot, and Relief Battalion Headquarters. Movement along the valley corridor and riverside approaches is constrained by debris, unstable embankments, and security threats. Engineer and security elements can clear and secure selected lanes, but doing so requires deliberate activation and support.\n\nMission:\nMove life-saving relief supplies from depots to designated receiving units during the current operational period, maximizing demand fulfillment while minimizing total operational cost (sum of transport costs, shortage penalties, per-vehicle deployment costs, and fixed route security risk costs).\n\nExecution/Concept:\n- Sources and limits: Each depot will issue commodities up to its available stock by supply type and day. No depot may dispatch more than it holds.\n- Demand and shortfalls: Each receiving unit has stated requirements by supply type. Controlled shortfalls are permitted only where unavoidable; any unmet demand incurs a penalty that reflects operational impact and will be used sparingly.\n- Movement and capacity: Shipments are consolidated into vehicle loads. Each supply type has a defined volumetric footprint. The total loaded volume moved from any depot to any unit may not exceed the aggregate carrying capacity of vehicles assigned to that lane.\n- Route activation and risk: Vehicles may only travel on lanes that are formally activated. Critically, activating any depot-to-unit route (sending any vehicles on that lane) incurs a fixed Risk Cost representing security overhead (escort teams, route clearance, coordination), regardless of how many vehicles subsequently use the lane. This is separate from per-vehicle deployment cost and per-lane transport cost.\n- Fleet management: Vehicle assignments across all routes are capped by the available fleet. Each activated lane can carry only the number of vehicles assigned to it, and the sum of vehicles across all lanes may not exceed the fleet total.\n- Cost drivers: Transport costs vary by lane and reflect distance, terrain, and congestion. Vehicle deployment has a per-vehicle cost. Unmet demand carries a penalty cost by supply and destination. Fixed Risk Costs apply to each lane that is activated, independent of volume moved.\n- Prioritization: Medical and water deliveries to life-threatening gaps are prioritized. Lane activation decisions will weigh the fixed risk overhead against the marginal benefit of opening additional corridors. Where multiple depots can serve a unit, allocate flows to minimize total cost while respecting stocks and capacities.\n- Command and control: The Logistics Coordination Cell will publish a convoy tasking order identifying activated routes, vehicle allocations by lane, and commodity loading plans. Security and engineer teams will align with activated lanes for clearance and escort; lanes not activated will not receive traffic.\n\nAnnex A - Logistics Data:\n{DEMANDS}\n{DEPOT_SUPPLIES}\n{TRANSPORT_COSTS}\n{RISK_COSTS}\n{PENALTY_COSTS}\n{SUPPLY_VOLUMES}\nFleet Parameters: Cost {VEHICLE_COST}, Capacity {VEHICLE_CAPACITY}, Total {TOTAL_VEHICLES}"
